{"name":"Heavy Gluten","hex":"#ddccaa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"heavy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having great weight.","Heavyset: overweight."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In a heavy manner; weightily; heavily; gravely.","To a great degree; greatly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heavy"},{"term":"gluten","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fibrin (formerly considered as one of the \"animal humours\"). .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any gluey, sticky substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gluten"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gluten","blocks":[{"text":"Gluten is a structural protein complex naturally found in certain cereal grains. The term gluten usually refers to the elastic network of a wheat grain's proteins, gliadin and glutenin primarily, which forms readily with the addition of water and often kneading in the case of bread dough. The types of grains that contain gluten include all species of wheat, barley, rye, and some cultivars of oat; moreover, cross hybrids of any of these cereal grains also contain gluten, e.g. triticale. Gluten makes up 75–85% of the total protein in bread wheat."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Gluten_Sources.png/330px-Gluten_Sources.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gluten","wikidata":"Q188251"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Pain_sans_gluten_%C3%A0_la_farine_de_ch%C3%A2taigne_et_charcuterie_corse.jpg/500px-Pain_sans_gluten_%C3%A0_la_farine_de_ch%C3%A2taigne_et_charcuterie_corse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Pain_sans_gluten_%C3%A0_la_farine_de_ch%C3%A2taigne_et_charcuterie_corse.jpg/1280px-Pain_sans_gluten_%C3%A0_la_farine_de_ch%C3%A2taigne_et_charcut